The length, norm, or magnitude of a column vector \[X = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{bmatrix}\] in Euclidean space is denoted by \(\|X\|\) (or \(|X|\)) and is algebraically defined as: \[\|X\| = \sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2}\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1</div>Let \(P\) be a standard transformation matrix parameterized by \(\theta\): \[P = \begin{bmatrix} \cos\theta &amp; \sin\theta \\ -\sin\theta &amp; \cos\theta \end{bmatrix}\] \[PX = \begin{bmatrix} \cos\theta &amp; \sin\theta \\ -\sin\theta &amp; \cos\theta \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} x_1\cos\theta + x_2\sin\theta \\ -x_1\sin\theta + x_2\cos\theta \end{bmatrix}\] \[\|PX\| = \sqrt{\left(x_1\cos\theta + x_2\sin\theta\right)^2 + \left(-x_1\sin\theta + x_2\cos\theta\right)^2}\] \[\begin{aligned}
    \|PX\| &amp;= \sqrt{\left(x_1^2\cos^2\theta + x_2^2\sin^2\theta + 2x_1x_2\cos\theta\sin\theta\right) + \left(x_1^2\sin^2\theta + x_2^2\cos^2\theta - 2x_1x_2\sin\theta\cos\theta\right)} \\
    &amp;= \sqrt{x_1^2\left(\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta\right) + x_2^2\left(\sin^2\theta + \cos^2\theta\right)}
\end{aligned}\] \[\|PX\| = \sqrt{x_1^2(1) + x_2^2(1)} = \sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2}\] Since \(\sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2} = \|X\|\), we establish that: \[\|PX\| = \|X\|\] This shows that multiplying by an orthogonal matrix preserves the length of the vector for any arbitrary vector \(X\), which matches statement (B).</div> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2</div>Alternatively, we can prove this coordinate-free length preservation property using matrix transpose identities. The norm squared of the transformed vector \(PX\) can be written using inner products as: \[\|PX\|^2 = (PX)^{\top}(PX)\] \[\|PX\|^2 = X^{\top} P^{\top} P X\] \[\|PX\|^2 = X^{\top} (I) X = X^{\top}X\] Since \(X^{\top}X = \|X\|^2\), taking the square root of both sides gives: \[\|PX\| = \|X\|\]</div> \[\boxed{\text{(B) $\|P\vec{x}\| = \|\vec{x}\|$ for all vectors $\vec{x}$}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Length Preservation (Isometry):</strong> A linear transformation preserves vector length (\(\|PX\| = \|X\|\)) if and only if its transformation matrix \(P\) is orthogonal (\(P^{\top}P = I\)).</li><li><strong>Orthogonal Transformation Dynamics:</strong> Geometric transformations like pure rotations and reflections are modeled by orthogonal matrices. They alter a vector's orientation while keeping its magnitude constant.</li></ul></div>

The input signal is \[x(t)=e^{-\alpha t}\sin(\omega t).\] Express the input as the imaginary part of a complex exponential: \[x(t)=\Im\left\{e^{(-\alpha+j\omega)t}ight\}.\] For an LTI system, the complex exponential \[e^{st}\] is an eigenfunction. Hence, \[e^{st}\longrightarrow H(s)e^{st},\] where \(H(s)\) is the system transfer function evaluated at \(s=-\alpha+j\omega\). Therefore, \[y(t)=|H(s)|\,e^{-\alpha t}
\sin\!\left(\omega t+\angle H(s)ight).\] Comparing with \[Ke^{- eta t}\sin(
u t+\phi),\] we obtain \[ eta=\alpha,\qquad 
u=\omega.\] \[ oxed{	ext{(c) } eta=\alpha 	ext{ and } 
u=\omega}\]

Let the Fourier series coefficients of \(x(t)\) be \(a_k\). Using the time-shifting property, \[x(t-t_0)\;\Longleftrightarrow\;a_k e^{-jk\omega_0t_0},\] \[x(t+t_0)\;\Longleftrightarrow\;a_k e^{jk\omega_0t_0},\] where \[\omega_0=\frac{2\pi}{T}.\] The Fourier series coefficients of \[y(t)=x(t-t_0)+x(t+t_0)\] are \[b_k=a_k\left(e^{-jk\omega_0t_0}+e^{jk\omega_0t_0}\right)
=2a_k\cos(k\omega_0t_0).\] Given that \[b_k=0,\qquad \forall\ \text{odd }k,\] and \(a_k\) need not be zero, \[\cos(k\omega_0t_0)=0,\qquad \forall\ \text{odd }k.\] Taking \(k=1\), \[\omega_0t_0=\frac{\pi}{2}\] (the smallest positive solution). Hence, \[t_0=\frac{\pi}{2\omega_0}
=\frac{\pi}{2(2\pi/T)}
=\frac{T}{4}.\] For every odd \(k\), \[\cos\!\left(k\frac{\pi}{2}\right)=0,\] so the condition is satisfied. \[\boxed{\text{(b) }t_0=\dfrac{T}{4}}\]

The input and impulse response are \[x(t)=\operatorname{sinc}(\alpha t),\qquad
h(t)=\operatorname{sinc}(\beta t).\] Using the Fourier transform pair, \[\operatorname{sinc}(at)
\Longleftrightarrow
\frac{1}{|a|}
\operatorname{rect}\!\left(\frac{\omega}{2\pi a}\right).\] Hence, \[X(\omega)=\frac{1}{|\alpha|}
\operatorname{rect}\!\left(\frac{\omega}{2\pi\alpha}\right),\] \[H(\omega)=\frac{1}{|\beta|}
\operatorname{rect}\!\left(\frac{\omega}{2\pi\beta}\right).\] The output spectrum is \[Y(\omega)=X(\omega)H(\omega).\] The product of two rectangular functions is another rectangular function whose bandwidth is the smaller of the two: \[Y(\omega)
=K\,
\operatorname{rect}\!\left(
\frac{\omega}{2\pi\,\min(\alpha,\beta)}
\right),\] where \(K\) is a constant. Taking the inverse Fourier transform, \[y(t)
=
K\,\operatorname{sinc}\!\left(\min(\alpha,\beta)t\right).\] Therefore, \[\boxed{\text{(a) }K\,\operatorname{sinc}(\gamma t),\ \gamma=\min(\alpha,\beta)}\]

The inverse Z-transform using the residue method is \[x[n]=\sum \operatorname{Res}\left\{X(z)z^{n-1}\right\}.\] Given, \[X(z)=\frac{z}{(z-a)^2}.\] Hence, \[X(z)z^{n-1}
=\frac{z^n}{(z-a)^2}.\] Since \(z=a\) is a double pole, \[\operatorname{Res}\left\{\frac{z^n}{(z-a)^2}\right\}_{z=a}
=
\left.\frac{d}{dz}\left(z^n\right)\right|_{z=a}.\] Now, \[\frac{d}{dz}\left(z^n\right)=nz^{n-1}.\] Therefore, \[\operatorname{Res}\left\{X(z)z^{n-1}\right\}_{z=a}
=na^{\,n-1}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(d) }na^{\,n-1}.}\]

