The polarity of induced emf is determined based on Lenz's law. Current must be induced in secondary in such a way that it opposes primary flux. Hence secondary flux must be anti-clockwise and current must come out of terminal \(R\). Since secondary delivers power to the load, current must leave the positive terminal. Hence, \(R\) is a positive terminal and the polarities are correctly marked. For \(0&lt;t&lt;1\) \[\frac{d\phi}{dt}=\frac{0.12}{1}
=0.12\,\mathrm{Wb/s}\] \[e_{rs}=N\frac{d\phi}{dt}\] \[e_{rs}=200\times0.12
=24\,\mathrm{V}\]

Slip for maximum torque is \[s_{m}=\frac{R_{r}'}{X_{s}+X_{r}'}\] \[s_{m}=\frac{1}{1.5+1.5}=\frac{1}{3}\] When frequency is changed, maximum torque occurs at starting. Hence, \[s_{m}=1\] Therefore, \[\frac{R_{r}'}{2\pi f\left(L_{s}+L_{r}'\right)}=1
\tag{1}\] Initially, at \(f=50\,\text{Hz}\), \[\frac{R_{r}'}{2\pi(50)\left(L_{s}+L_{r}'\right)}=\frac{1}{3}
\tag{2}\] Dividing equation (2) by equation (1), \[\frac{f}{50}=\frac{1}{3}\] \[f=16.67\,\text{Hz}\] Due to constant \(\dfrac{V}{f}\) control, \[\frac{400}{50}=\frac{V}{16.67}\] \[V=\frac{400}{3}=133.33\,\text{V}\] \[\boxed{B}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Slip at maximum torque is \(s_{m}=\dfrac{R_{r}'}{X_{s}+X_{r}'}\).</li><li>Reactance is directly proportional to frequency.</li><li>For maximum torque at starting, \(s_{m}=1\).</li><li>In constant \(V/f\) control, voltage is reduced in the same ratio as frequency.</li></ul></div>

