=
\frac{90}{1500}
=
\frac{3}{50}\] For an inverted induction motor, the rotor moves opposite to the magnetic field. Therefore, the correct answer is \[\boxed{D}\]

Detent torque is the torque present in a stepper motor when the phase windings are not excited. It is produced due to the interaction between the permanent magnet rotor and the toothed stator structure. Detent torque represents the maximum torque that must be applied to move the rotor away from its preferred resting position when the windings are unexcited. Hence, \[\text{Detent Torque}
=
\text{Maximum static torque with phase winding unexcited}\] Therefore, the correct answer is \[\boxed{\text{ D}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Detent torque exists only in permanent-magnet and hybrid stepper motors.</li><li>It is present even when no current flows in the phase windings.</li><li>Detent torque is caused by rotor permanent magnets interacting with stator teeth.</li><li>Holding torque is measured with the phase windings excited, whereas detent torque is measured with the windings unexcited.</li></ul></div>

