The line-end voltages have equal magnitudes but are displaced by \[180^\circ\] which corresponds to a severe power-transfer condition. The minimum three-phase fault current is given to be \[I_{f,\min}
=
5 I_{L,\max}\] The directional overcurrent relay pickup setting is \[I_{\text{pickup}}
=
1.25 I_{L,\max}\] Since \[I_{f,\min}
=
5 I_{L,\max}
&gt;
1.25 I_{L,\max}\] the directional overcurrent relay will operate. Pilot relaying schemes compare current directions or phase relationships at both line ends and are designed to operate for internal faults. Hence both directional-comparison and segregated phase-comparison pilot schemes will pick up. For a mho relay, operation depends on the apparent impedance seen by the relay. With equal terminal voltages and a phase displacement of \[180^\circ\] the apparent impedance trajectory may not enter the Zone-1 mho characteristic even when Zone-1 is set to \[80\%\] of the line impedance. Therefore, the scheme that will not pick up is \[\boxed{\text{(A) Distance protection using mho relay with Zone-1 set to 80\%}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Mho relays operate based on apparent impedance seen by the relay.</li><li>Directional overcurrent relays operate when current exceeds the pickup setting in the correct direction.</li><li>Pilot protection schemes use information from both line ends and are highly sensitive to internal faults.</li><li>Relay operation depends on the impedance trajectory relative to the relay characteristic.</li></ul></div>

Load current is \[I_o=10\,\text{A}\] Each phase of the source conducts for \(240^\circ\) out of \(360^\circ\). Hence, the rms value of the source current is \[I_s=I_o\sqrt{\frac{240}{360}}\] \[I_s=10\sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}=8.165\,\text{A}\] The rms value of the fundamental component of the source current is \[I_{s1}=\frac{4I_o}{\sqrt{2}\pi}\sin\frac{\pi}{3}\] \[I_{s1}
=\frac{4\times10}{\sqrt{2}\pi}\times\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}
=7.8\,\text{A}\] The total harmonic distortion is \[\mathrm{THD}
=\sqrt{\left(\frac{I_s}{I_{s1}}\right)^2-1}\times100\%\] \[=\sqrt{\left(\frac{8.165}{7.8}\right)^2-1}\times100\%
=31\%\] \[\boxed{B}\]

Given, \[p(\omega_1 t)=A\sin\omega_1 t\] applied to the X-input and the unknown signal \(q(\omega_2 t)\) applied to the Y-input. The CRO is in X--Y mode and shows a Lissajous figure. <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> For Lissajous figures, \[\frac{f_x}{f_y}=\frac{\text{Number of tangencies to vertical sides}}{\text{Number of tangencies to horizontal sides}}\] From the figure, the horizontal lines cut the curve at 2 points and vertical line at 4 points. Hence, \[\frac{f_x}{f_y}=\frac{4}{2}
\Rightarrow
f_y=\frac{f_x}{2}\] Therefore, \[\omega_2=\frac{\omega_1}{2}\] The figure is symmetric about both axes and corresponds to a signal in quadrature phase. Since a cosine wave is a sine wave shifted by \(90^\circ\), \[q(\omega_2 t)=A\cos\omega_2 t\] Therefore, the correct option is \[\boxed{\textbf{D. }q(\omega_2 t)=A\cos\omega_2 t,\qquad \omega_2=\frac{\omega_1}{2}}\]
